---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Cmd from "../components/Cmd.astro";
import Contracts from "../components/Contracts.astro";
import Onward from "../components/Onward.astro";
import Snippet from "../components/Snippet.astro";
import { REPO, install, levels, roadmap, roadmapIntro, samples, site } from "../lib/site";
import { SOFTWARE_ID, softwareNode } from "../lib/schema";

// THE INDEX CARRIES THE BRAND TERM AND NOTHING ELSE IS BUILT AROUND IT.
// Somebody who types "Terraken" already knows the name; somebody who types
// "terraform moved block" does not, and there are far more of the second. So
// this page is what the tool is, what it prints, what it will not do and how to
// install it, and the five guide pages carry the search demand. Do not move a
// moved-block, taint or forced-replacement explanation onto this page: each
// would compete with the page built to answer that query.
//
// THE ORDER OF THE SECTIONS IS AN ARGUMENT. Output, then the boundaries that
// make the output trustworthy, then how to get it, then how it ranks, then the
// source. The boundaries used to be second to last, which is where a claim
// goes when it is being treated as a footnote; they are the answer to the
// question a reader has immediately after seeing what the tool prints.
---

<Base
  title="Terraken - everything you can know about a Terraform change"
  description="A free command-line tool that reads a Terraform or OpenTofu plan and ranks the change by how much damage it can do. No credentials, no network, no values."
  mainEntityId={SOFTWARE_ID}
  schema={[softwareNode()]}
>
  {/* THE HERO SHOWS THE TOOL WORKING. IT DOES NOT RESTATE THE MASTHEAD.
      (Dan, 16 Sep 2026.)

      It used to open with the hostname as a kicker, then the mark and the
      wordmark as an h1 lockup - which put the same mark and the same word
      twice within 200px of each other, and told a reader the address of the
      page they were already on. Three restatements before a single claim.

      What is here instead is what DESIGN.md already calls for: "the captured
      output is the hero asset on every page". The claim is the h1, because the
      name is in the bar directly above and the page's job is to say what the
      thing does; the install command sits in the hero so somebody already
      convinced never has to go looking for it; and the report is the proof,
      on the ink it came off.

      DO NOT PUT THE WORDMARK BACK IN HERE while it is also in the masthead. */}
  <section class="section section--tight hero band-ink">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-say">
          <h1>{site.tagline}</h1>
          <p class="lead">{site.lead}</p>
          <div class="hero-get">
            <Cmd cmd={install.go} />
          </div>
          <p class="hero-meta">
            <span class="mono-label">Go</span>
            <span class="mono-label">MIT</span>
            <span class="mono-label">{install.platforms}</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-show">
          <Snippet
            kind="output"
            label="terraken plan.json"
            caption="Terraken ranking a plan with one critical finding"
            html={samples.heroCritical}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* THE SAMPLE IS NOT REPEATED HERE. It is in the hero, one scroll above, and
      showing the same capture twice was the other half of the duplication Dan
      called out. This section is what that report means and what else could
      have appeared in it, so the three notes now read against the hero rather
      than against a copy of it - which is why the first one says "above". */}
  <section class="section section--tight section--plane">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="block-h">Reading the report</h2>
      <div class="reading">
        <p>
          One section per severity present, most severe first, and none at all
          for a severity with nothing in it. The report is set to the width of
          your terminal, between 60 and 100 columns.
        </p>
        <p>
          The finding above is critical rather than high for one reason: the
          resource type holds data, so destroying it loses that data. It is the
          only escalation in the tool, and it applies to destruction alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notice what is not there. The attribute that forced the replacement is
          named as <code>zone</code>, and neither its old value nor its new one
          appears, because no value ever does.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="tells-h">What it finds</h3>
      <ul class="tells" role="list">
        <li><strong>What this change destroys</strong>, ranked, with the ones that lose data first</li>
        <li><strong>Why</strong> a resource is being replaced, using Terraform's own stated reason</li>
        <li><strong>Which attribute</strong> forced the replacement, by name</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Renames that forgot a <code>moved</code> block</strong>: a destroy and a
          create that look like the same resource, which is how a refactor quietly
          destroys a database it meant to keep
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Attributes whose before and after are the same value written
          differently</strong>: a reshuffled list, a JSON policy whose keys moved,
          a re-indented heredoc, a port that came back as a string, a null that
          became an empty list
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>When that is the whole of a resource's change</strong>, said out loud,
          which is the fastest way to clear an update in place that is really nothing
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What cannot be known until apply</strong>, so you can see which claims
          about this change are unverifiable in review
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="tells-note">
        Each of those is a statement of fact rather than a verdict. Order is
        significant for a container command, whitespace is significant in a
        script, and a tool that called either one harmless would eventually be
        confidently wrong about somebody's production change. Terraken says what
        kind of difference it found and leaves the call to you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* THE BOUNDARIES SIT BESIDE THE OUTPUT, NOT AT THE FOOT OF THE PAGE.
      A reader's second question after "what does it print" is "what did it
      have to touch to print that", and the answer is the reason the tool is
      safe to point at a plan nobody has vetted. It used to be the second to
      last section, which is where a claim goes when it is being treated as a
      footnote rather than as the evidence it is. */}
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <Contracts />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* IMMEDIATELY AFTER THE CONTRACTS, ON PURPOSE. The adjacency is the whole
      argument: the contracts are not a list of things the tool refuses to do,
      they are the filter that produced this roadmap. Move this section
      anywhere else and it reads as an ordinary coming-soon list, which is the
      one thing it must not read as.

      Everything here is unshipped. The kicker says so, every item carries its
      issue number, and tests/content.test.mjs fails the build if either stops
      being true. */}
  <section class="section section--tight section--plane" data-roadmap>
    <div class="container">
      <p class="kicker">{roadmapIntro.kicker}</p>
      <h2 class="block-h">{roadmapIntro.h}</h2>
      <p class="roadmap-intro">{roadmapIntro.p}</p>
      <ul class="roadmap">
        {roadmap.map((r) => (
          <li class="roadmap-item">
            <h3 class="roadmap-h">
              <a href={`${REPO}/issues/${r.issue}`}
                >{r.h}<span class="out-glyph" aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span><span
                  class="sr-only"
                >
                  (external site)</span
                ></a
              >
              <span class="roadmap-issue">#{r.issue}</span>
            </h3>
            <p class="roadmap-p">{r.p}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="roadmap-outro">{roadmapIntro.outro}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="block-h">Install</h2>
      <div class="install-grid">
        <div>
          <Cmd cmd={install.go} />
          {/* The `&#32;` is load-bearing: the compiler strips the whitespace
              between a text node and an element on the next source line, so
              without it this reads "from thereleases page". */}
          <p class="install-more">
            Or download a binary from the&#32;
            <a href={install.releases}>releases page<span class="out-glyph" aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span><span class="sr-only"> (external site)</span></a>.
            The release ships {install.binaries}, built from the same package, so
            use whichever name is free on your machine. Builds are for {install.platforms}.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="use-h">Then run it</h3>
          {install.use.map((c) => <Cmd cmd={c} />)}
          <p class="install-more">
            Or skip the file entirely. Plan JSON can hold a credential in the
            clear, and a file on a CI runner is one more place for it to be
            left behind:
          </p>
          <Cmd cmd={install.pipe} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* --ruled because Install above is now a ground section too, and a plane
      draws the edge for its neighbours while a ground section does not. The
      roadmap section taking the plane treatment is what pushed Install off it. */}
  <section class="section section--tight section--ruled">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="block-h">Four levels, and no medium</h2>
      <p class="levels-intro">
        A middle bucket is where findings go to be ignored, so there is not one.
      </p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">What reaches it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {levels.map((l) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code class={`lvl lvl--${l.name}`}>{l.name}</code></td>
                <td>{l.what}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight section--plane">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="source">
        <h2 class="block-h">The source, and the tests that hold it</h2>
        <p class="source-p">
          Every line is public and MIT licensed, and the three boundaries above
          are held by tests in that repository rather than by a promise on this
          website. It also carries the fixtures the output on this site was
          produced from, including a plan that exists only to be leaked from.
        </p>
        <p class="source-links">
          <a class="source-out" href={REPO}>
            github.com/dbhq-uk/terraken
            <span class="out-glyph" aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span>
            <span class="sr-only"> (external site)</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* Every other page on the site. This one is the hub, so it is the one
          place the whole list belongs; the guides carry three each, chosen for
          the reader who got to the foot of that particular page. */}
      <Onward
        heading="The guides"
        links={[
          {
            href: "/terraform-moved-block/",
            label: "Terraform moved blocks",
            desc: "What a moved block is, why a rename destroys without one, and how to check the plan afterwards.",
          },
          {
            href: "/terraform-rename-resource/",
            label: "Renaming a resource",
            desc: "Inside a module, between modules, and when a for_each key changes.",
          },
          {
            href: "/terraform-state-mv/",
            label: "state mv and state rm",
            desc: "The two state commands, the two blocks that replace them, and what each one leaves behind.",
          },
          {
            href: "/terraform-taint/",
            label: "taint and untaint",
            desc: "What tainted means in state, and the apply option that superseded the command.",
          },
          {
            href: "/terraform-forces-replacement/",
            label: "Forced replacement",
            desc: "Why a plan says forces replacement, and how to find the attribute that caused it.",
          },
          {
            href: "/docs/",
            label: "Docs",
            desc: "Every flag, the four output formats, the JSON shape and the exit codes.",
          },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Deeper than the band it replaced. An ink field needs room around what is in
     it or it reads as a bar rather than as a ground. */
  .hero { padding-block: clamp(56px, 8vw, 88px) clamp(48px, 6vw, 76px); }

  /* Say it on the left, show it on the right. The sample column is a fixed
     ceiling rather than a fraction, for the same reason the old .split was:
     the capture is 633px at the width the binary set it to, and a fraction of
     the container lands under that on a 1440px screen, so the block scrolls
     sideways - the one thing DESIGN.md says a terminal block must never do.

     Stacks below 1100px with the words FIRST. That is the opposite of the old
     .split rule, and deliberately: this is the top of the page, so a reader
     arriving on a phone should meet the claim before the evidence. Lower down,
     where the sample was the subject of its own section, the sample led. */
  .hero-grid {
    display: grid;
    /* The sample column is a ceiling, not a fraction, and the words take what
       is left rather than a matching half - a 660px terminal beside a 660px
       column of prose makes the prose a wall. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 620px);
    gap: clamp(32px, 4vw, 64px);
    align-items: center;
  }
  .hero-grid > * { min-width: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
    .hero-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 40px); }
  }

  /* THE CLAIM IS THE H1 NOW, AND IT NEEDS ITS OWN SIZE. global.css sets h1 at
     up to 3.2rem, which was chosen for the one-word product name that used to
     sit here. Applied to a 64-character sentence inside a half-width column it
     broke into seven lines of three words and swallowed the hero.

     Smaller and with a wider measure: 2.5rem at the top end against a 26ch
     column gives three lines, which is a headline rather than a poster. */
  .hero-say h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 2.5vw, 2.5rem);
    max-width: 26ch;
    line-height: 1.12;
  }
  .hero .lead { margin-top: clamp(16px, 2vw, 22px); max-width: 54ch; }
  .hero-get { margin-top: clamp(22px, 2.6vw, 30px); }
  /* The Cmd component is built for paper. On ink its surface and border both
     disappear, so they are restated here rather than a tone prop being added
     to a component used in five other places on light ground. */
  /* A COMMAND, NOT A FORM FIELD. The Cmd component is built for paper: a white
     box with a light border beside a solid white button. Dropped onto the ink
     hero that reads as an input with a submit next to it, which is what "looks
     amateur" was about.

     The treatment here is the one every well-made CLI site uses - bun.sh is the
     clearest example. The command sits on the ground it came from, carries a
     prompt character so it reads as something you type, and the copy
     affordance is quiet until you want it. No white anywhere. */
  .hero-get :global(.cmd-row) {
    position: relative;
    background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.035);
    border: 1px solid var(--ink-line);
    border-radius: var(--r-sm);
    padding: 0.85em 1em;
    gap: 0;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .hero-get :global(.cmd-text) {
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    color: var(--ink-text);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }
  /* The prompt. A $ is what the reader sees in their own terminal, and it is
     the single cheapest signal that this line is a command rather than a
     value to be filled in. aria-hidden and unselectable, so copying the line
     by hand does not take the $ with it. */
  .hero-get :global(.cmd-text)::before {
    content: "$";
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-text) 45%, var(--ink));
    margin-right: 0.75em;
    user-select: none;
  }
  /* Ghost, not solid. It brightens on hover and on keyboard focus, so it is
     discoverable without competing with the command for attention - and blue
     is spent on the one primary action elsewhere, never here. */
  .hero-get :global(.install-copy) {
    background: none;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-text) 60%, var(--ink));
    min-height: 32px;
    padding: 0 .7em;
    margin-left: .75em;
  }
  .hero-get :global(.install-copy:hover),
  .hero-get :global(.install-copy:focus-visible) {
    color: var(--surface);
    border-color: var(--ink-line);
    background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.06);
  }
  /* WRAPS RATHER THAN SCROLLS, here only. The install line is 56 characters
     and the hero column is narrower than that in mono, so it arrived with a
     scrollbar and the reader saw "go install github.com/dbhq-uk/ter". A
     half-shown command reads as a broken one - and there is a Copy button
     beside it, so nobody is typing it out of the box anyway. Lower down the
     page the Install section is full width and its commands still sit on one
     line, which is why this is scoped to the hero. */
  .hero-get :global(.cmd-text) {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    overflow-x: visible;
  }
  .hero-get :global(.cmd-note) { color: var(--ink-text); opacity: .75; }
  .hero-get :global(code) { color: var(--ink-text); }
  .hero-meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 8px 18px; margin-top: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 26px); }

  /* The sample sits on ink inside an ink band, so its own --ink background is
     invisible and the hairline is doing all the work of saying where the
     terminal starts. Lifted a touch off the field so it reads as a block on
     the ground rather than a hole in it. */
  .hero-show :global(.snip--output .snip-pre) {
    background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.03);
    border-color: #27303E;
  }
  .hero-show :global(.snip-label) { color: var(--ink-text); opacity: .7; }

  .reading { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 40px); margin-top: 10px; }
  .reading p { line-height: 1.7; }
  .reading code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .88em; padding: .12em .34em; border-radius: var(--r-xs); background: var(--surface-2); color: var(--text); }


  /* The hinge between the sample and the list, inside one section. It is a
     sub-heading rather than a second h2 because the two are one subject: the
     block above is the shape of the report and the list below is what can be
     in it. */
  .tells-h { margin-top: clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 56px); margin-bottom: clamp(16px, 2vw, 22px); }
  .tells { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; max-width: 780px; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .tells li { padding-block: clamp(12px, 1.5vw, 15px); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); color: var(--text-2); line-height: 1.6; }
  .tells li:first-child { border-top: 0; padding-top: 0; }
  .tells strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  .tells code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .88em; padding: .12em .34em; border-radius: var(--r-xs); background: var(--surface-2); color: var(--text); }
  .tells-note { margin-top: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 26px); max-width: 68ch; line-height: 1.7; }

  .install-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px); max-width: 980px; align-items: start; }
  .use-h { margin-bottom: 14px; }
  .install-more { margin-top: 14px; font-size: .88rem; line-height: 1.6; max-width: 52ch; }
  .install-more a { color: var(--blue-deep); font-weight: 600; }
  .install-more a:hover { color: var(--text); }

  .levels-intro { max-width: 60ch; }
  /* The level names carry the severity colour the terminal prints them in, so
     the table and the output agree. Text, never a background tint: a coloured
     block behind a word is a badge, and these are values in a table. */
  .lvl { font-weight: 600; }
  .lvl--critical { color: var(--danger); }
  .lvl--high { color: var(--warn); }
  .lvl--low { color: var(--text-2); }
  .lvl--info { color: var(--text-3); }

  /* The roadmap. Two columns on desktop because ten items in one column reads
     as a backlog dump; the grid makes it a map. Deliberately quieter than the
     shipped sections above it - --text-2 body, no accent fill, no card - so it
     cannot be mistaken at a glance for a list of things the tool does. */
  .roadmap-intro { max-width: 68ch; margin-top: 10px; line-height: 1.7; }
  .roadmap {
    list-style: none; padding: 0;
    margin: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 40px) 0 0;
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(22px, 2.6vw, 34px) clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px);
  }
  .roadmap-item { border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-top: 14px; }
  .roadmap-h {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 8px;
    font-size: 1rem; letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  .roadmap-h a { color: var(--text); }
  .roadmap-h a:hover { color: var(--blue-deep); }
  /* The issue number is the honesty marker: every item points at an open issue
     rather than at a page describing a feature. Monospace so it reads as a
     reference and not as a version. */
  .roadmap-issue { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .78rem; color: var(--text-3); font-weight: 500; }
  .roadmap-p { font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--text-2); margin: 0; }
  .roadmap-outro { margin-top: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 40px); max-width: 68ch; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-2); font-size: .94rem; }

  .source { max-width: 780px; }
  .source-p { margin-top: 10px; max-width: 66ch; line-height: 1.7; }
  .source-links { margin-top: 18px; }
  .source-out { color: var(--blue-deep); font-weight: 600; font-size: .96rem; }
  .source-out:hover { color: var(--text); }
</style>
